examples.question_text:
The graph of $f(x)$ is shown. Sketch $y=1/f(x)$. Label the asymptote and $y$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-2.5,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4.5)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-2,2,4} {\draw (\t,-0.1)--(\t,0.1);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2} {\draw (-0.1,\t)--(0.1,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->] (-2,-4)--(4.5,2.5);
\fill (2,0) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below right,yshift=-8pt] at (2,0) {$(2,0)$};
\node[above left,text=curveBlue] at (4,2) {$f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The zero of $f$ gives the vertical asymptote $x=2$. The horizontal asymptote is $y=0$.
The invariant points are $(1,-1)$ and $(3,1)$. Reciprocating the ordinate at $x=0$ gives the $y$-intercept $(0,-\frac12)$.
The reciprocal is negative for $x<2$ and positive for $x>2$. It decreases on each branch, approaching $-\infty$ as $x\to2^-$ and $+\infty$ as $x\to2^+$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-2.5,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (2,-3)--(2,3) node[above right] {$x=2$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-2:1.65,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=2.35:4.7,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
\fill (0,-0.5) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below left,xshift=-5pt] at (0,-0.5) {$(0,-\frac12)$};
\fill (1,-1) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below left] at (1,-1) {$(1,-1)$};
\fill (3,1) circle (1.4pt);
\node[above right] at (3,1) {$(3,1)$};
\node[below] at (4.4,0) {$y=0$};
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (-1,0.8) {$1/f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph of $f(x)=(x-1)^2-4$ is shown. Find the turning point of $y=1/f(x)$ and sketch its graph, labelling its asymptotes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-2.5,0)--(4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4.8)--(0,4.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-1.9:3.9,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x-1)*(\x-1)-4});
\foreach \t in {-1,3} {\fill (\t,0) circle (1.4pt);\node[above left] at (\t,0) {$(\t,0)$};}
\fill (1,-4) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below right] at (1,-4) {$(1,-4)$};
\node[above right,text=curveBlue] at (3.4,1.8) {$f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The zeros at $x=-1$ and $x=3$ give vertical asymptotes. Since $f(x)\to+\infty$ at both ends, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0$, approached from above.
The minimum $(1,-4)$ becomes the maximum $(1,-\frac14)$.
The reciprocal is positive outside the two zeros and negative between them. At $x=-1$, its left branch approaches $+\infty$ and its right branch approaches $-\infty$; at $x=3$, these behaviours reverse.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-3,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6)--(0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-1,-2.4)--(-1,2.4) node[above left] {$x=-1$};
\draw[dashed] (3,-2.4)--(3,2.4) node[above right] {$x=3$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-2.8:-1.11,samples=120] plot (\x,{1/((\x-1)*(\x-1)-4)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-0.89:2.89,samples=180] plot (\x,{1/((\x-1)*(\x-1)-4)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=3.11:4.8,samples=120] plot (\x,{1/((\x-1)*(\x-1)-4)});
\fill (1,-0.25) circle (1.4pt);
\draw[->] (1.7,-1.4)--(1.03,-0.29);
\node[below] at (1.7,-1.4) {$(1,-\frac14)$};
\node[below] at (4.3,0) {$y=0$};
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (1,1.4) {$1/f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The graph of $f(x)=(x-2)^2+2$ is shown. Find the turning point of $y=1/f(x)$ and sketch the reciprocal, labelling any asymptotes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-1,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-0.1:4.1,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x-2)*(\x-2)+2});
\fill (2,2) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below right] at (2,2) {$(2,2)$};
\node[above right,text=curveBlue] at (3.5,4.25) {$f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Reciprocate the turning-point ordinate: the minimum $(2,2)$ becomes the maximum $(2,\frac12)$.
There are no zeros, so there are no vertical asymptotes. The reciprocal remains positive and approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ at both ends.
Since $f$ decreases before $x=2$ and increases afterwards, its reciprocal increases before $x=2$ and decreases afterwards.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=30mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-1.5,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.15)--(0,1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-1.2:5.2,samples=120] plot (\x,{1/((\x-2)*(\x-2)+2)});
\fill (2,0.5) circle (1.4pt);
\node[above right] at (2,0.5) {$(2,\frac12)$};
\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$y=0$};
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (-0.7,0.45) {$1/f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Sketch the base parabola $f(x)=(x+2)^2$. Its zero at $x=-2$ gives the vertical asymptote $x=-2$. The invariant points are $(-3,1)$ and $(-1,1)$.
The reciprocal is positive on both sides. It approaches $+\infty$ as $x\to-2^-$ or $x\to-2^+$ and approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=0$ from above at both ends.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-5.5,0)--(1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2,-0.4)--(-2,3.3) node[above] {$x=-2$};
\draw[dashed,domain=-3.75:-0.25,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)*(\x+2)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-5:-2.56,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/((\x+2)*(\x+2))});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-1.44:1,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/((\x+2)*(\x+2))});
\fill (-3,1) circle (1.4pt);\node[left] at (-3,1) {$(-3,1)$};
\fill (-1,1) circle (1.4pt);\node[right] at (-1,1) {$(-1,1)$};
\node[left] at (-3.7,3) {$f(x)$};
\node[below] at (0.8,0) {$y=0$};
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (-4.5,1.9) {$1/f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) Sketch the base cubic $f(x)=(x+2)^3$. Again, the asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $y=0$. The invariant points are $(-3,-1)$ and $(-1,1)$.
The reciprocal is negative for $x<-2$ and positive for $x>-2$. It approaches $-\infty$ as $x\to-2^-$ and $+\infty$ as $x\to-2^+$. At the left and right ends it approaches $0^-$ and $0^+$ respectively.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-5.5,0)--(1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5)--(0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-2,-3.3)--(-2,3.3) node[above] {$x=-2$};
\draw[dashed,domain=-3.45:-0.55,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x+2)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-5:-2.69,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/((\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x+2))});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=-1.31:1,samples=100] plot (\x,{1/((\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x+2))});
\fill (-3,-1) circle (1.4pt);\node[below left] at (-3,-1) {$(-3,-1)$};
\fill (-1,1) circle (1.4pt);\node[above right] at (-1,1) {$(-1,1)$};
\node[right] at (-0.6,2.8) {$f(x)$};
\node[below] at (0.8,0) {$y=0$};
\node[above,text=curveBlue] at (-4.5,0.6) {$1/f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
In both diagrams, the dashed curve is the base function and the blue curve is its reciprocal.

$\begin{aligned}g(x)&=\frac1{f(x)}\\&=\frac1{\frac{x-2}{x+2}}\\&=\frac{x+2}{x-2}\end{aligned}$
Keep the original exclusion $x=-2$ and exclude the zero of $f$ at $x=2$. Thus the domain is $\mathbb R\setminus\{-2,2\}$.
The excluded input $x=-2$ gives a hole at $(-2,0)$, rather than an intercept. The zero at $x=2$ gives the vertical asymptote $x=2$. Since $f(x)\to1$ at both ends, $g(x)\to1$, so the horizontal asymptote is $y=1$.
The $y$-intercept is $(0,-1)$. The reciprocal is positive for $x<-2$, negative for $-2<x<2$ and positive for $x>2$. It approaches $-\infty$ as $x\to2^-$ and $+\infty$ as $x\to2^+$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (-5.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4.5)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (2,-4.3)--(2,5.3) node[above right] {$x=2$};
\draw[dashed] (-5.2,1)--(6.2,1) node[right] {$y=1$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<-,domain=-5.2:-2,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)/(\x-2)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,->,domain=-2:1.2,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)/(\x-2)});
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,domain=3:6.2,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)/(\x-2)});
\draw[fill=white] (-2,0) circle (2pt);
\node[below left,yshift=-5pt] at (-2,0) {$(-2,0)$};
\fill (0,-1) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below left] at (0,-1) {$(0,-1)$};
\node[above right,text=curveBlue] at (4,3) {$g(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
